---
/* The research map. David's Google My Map of the Croatian record books, rebuilt
   here so it survives the account, joined to what this archive has actually
   done with it. The map, the panel and the filters are the kit's Atlas — the
   same component the Atlas page uses, pointed at a different blob. Nothing in
   the kit is touched. */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Atlas from "@daviddef/archive-kit/components/Atlas.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import { md, mdp } from "../lib/md.js";
import r from "../data/researchmap.json";

const s = r.stats;
/* Three states have to be told apart at four pixels across, on a pale map,
   in a wash of a thousand markers. The archive's own palette is all mid-tone
   and they vanished into each other, so these are pulled apart on BOTH hue
   and lightness: cold pale grey for nothing, hot gold for the middle, deep
   green for done. */
const cats = [
  ["untouched", "Not walked — waypoint only",    "#93A1AD"],
  ["listed",    "Volumes catalogued",            "#E9A020"],
  ["read",      "Something here has been read",  "#12703F"],
];
const LAB = { rc: "Roman Catholic", orth: "Orthodox", gc: "Greek Catholic",
              jew: "Jewish", mil: "Military", ref: "Reformed", civil: "Civil",
              ev: "Evangelical" };
const layers = Object.entries(s.byLayer).sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1]);
const pct = (n) => ((100 * n) / s.places).toFixed(1);
---
<Base
  title="The Research Map"
  eyebrow={`${s.places} record-book places · ${s.volumes} volumes inventoried · ${s.read + s.listed} places walked`}
  dek="Every parish in Croatia whose registers are on film, and how far this archive has got with each one. Click a marker: it tells you what confession the books are, whether we hold the volume list, and where we hold it, it hands you the volumes themselves.">

  <Atlas
    dataUrl={u("/research-map-data.json")}
    cats={cats}
    bounds={[[42.2, 13.2], [46.8, 19.6]]}
    homeLabel="Back to Croatia"
    placeholder="Brinje · Svetvinčenat · Otočac · Vodnjan"
    hint={`${s.placed} of the ${s.places} places can be put on the map. ${s.read + s.listed} of them — ${pct(s.read + s.listed)}% — have been walked to volume level.`}
  />

  <h2 style="font-size:30px;margin-top:50px">Where this map comes from, and what it is worth</h2>

  <div class="one" style="border-left-color:var(--accent);margin-top:22px">
    <div class="lab">It is David's map, rebuilt because the original cannot be kept</div>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:8px" set:html={mdp(
`David built a **Google My Map** of the Croatian record books over several years — FamilySearch first, then the other confessions — and drew in a second layer of Croatian genealogists working their own parishes. **A My Map dies with the account**, and it cannot be crawled.

So it was exported and rebuilt here. **That export is where the trouble starts.** The KML holds **1,709 placemarks**, but only **247 of them carry coordinates** — and of the 1,196 record-book places, only **ninety** do. The rest are *address pins*, which Google geocodes when it draws the map and not before. **As a backup, the export is about eight per cent of a map.**

What every pin does carry is a **FamilySearch browse waypoint**: \`owc=9R2S-MJ7:391644801,392042201\` and so on, **1,194 of them**. That is the part that matters, because a waypoint is the key that opens a parish's shelf. The coordinates were rebuilt instead from the **GeoNames** country dumps for Croatia and its seven neighbours, which put **${s.placed} of the ${s.places}** places back on the map without a single geocoding call.

**${s.places - s.placed} places are still unplaced** — they are mostly compound parish names like *Split-Lučac* or *Sv. Jakov-Šiljevica* that no gazetteer carries as one string.`)}></div>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top:22px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px">
    <div class="one">
      <div class="lab">What the three colours mean — and what they do not</div>
      <div class="prose" style="margin-top:8px" set:html={mdp(
`**Not walked** is the honest state of most of this map: the archive holds the parish's waypoint and nothing else. It knows the books exist. It has never opened the shelf.

**Volumes catalogued** means the parish has been walked and its volume list is held here, with a link that opens each book at its first image. **It does not mean anybody has read them.**

**Something here has been read** means a row in the [search register](${"/searched/"}) names the place as a source. That flag is **derived by matching place names against the register's own source and destination fields** — never against its prose, which mentions places for all sorts of reasons. A handful of names that are also ordinary Croatian words or surnames are excluded by hand. **It is approximate, and it will be wrong in both directions until every row is tagged properly.**`)}></div>
    </div>

    <div class="one">
      <div class="lab">The number this page exists to show</div>
      <div class="prose" style="margin-top:8px" set:html={mdp(
`**${s.read + s.listed} of ${s.places}.** Nine per cent of the record-book places on this map have been walked far enough to know what volumes sit in them, and **${s.read}** have had anything actually read.

The walked part is the ground this family actually stands on, and all three regions are done: **Istria**, forty-three parishes and 263 volumes · **Kvarner**, forty parishes and 255 volumes · **Dalmatia**, sixty parishes and 343 volumes.

*This paragraph said something else for about an hour.* It reported that thirty-four of the forty Kvarner parishes had never been walked — **and they had been**. The builder was reading the wrong field: Kvarner keeps its volumes under a field called **films**, like Dalmatia, and carries a stale **books** title-list on six of its forty parishes. Reading **books** invented a gap that did not exist and under-counted the archive by thirty-four parishes and 201 volumes. [Logged as a correction](/corrections/).`)}></div>
    </div>

    <div class="one">
      <div class="lab">The confessions, as David separated them</div>
      <div class="prose" style="margin-top:8px" set:html={mdp(
`The map keeps his own layers, because the confession decides which archive holds the book and in what language it is written.

${layers.map(([k, n]) => `**${LAB[k] || k}** — ${n}`).join(" · ")}

Croatian registers are not all Catholic and a family that looks absent from one set can be sitting in another. **The ${s.byLayer.orth || 0} Orthodox parishes in particular are a body this archive has never touched.**`)}></div>
    </div>

    <div class="one">
      <div class="lab">And a layer that is not records at all</div>
      <div class="prose" style="margin-top:8px" set:html={mdp(
`His map carries a tenth layer of **${s.researchers} Croatian genealogists** — each with a researcher number, the parish they work, their confession, and the surnames they are chasing. It is a directory of living people working the same ground, and it is **not on this map**, because people are not record books and because the archive's rule on living people applies to strangers as much as to family.

It is kept in the data, unpublished, and the useful question it answers is narrow: *is anybody else working our parishes?*`)}></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:28px">Rebuilt from the KML export of <em>Genealogy Resources — Croatia &amp; Surrounds</em>, kept whole at <code>data/genealogy-resources-croatia.kml</code>. Coordinates from the GeoNames dumps for HR, BA, SI, RS, HU, IT, AT and ME. Volume inventories from this archive's own walks of the FamilySearch waypoints. <strong>Nothing on this map is a finding</strong> — it is a record of what exists and how far we have got.</p>
</Base>
